Dear Alvaro,

not sure I understand. You seem to have 5 years, each with 6 seasons, is this right ? What is your period over which you assume closure ? If you want to estimate seasonal occupancy probability, you need to have replicate observations WITHIN EACH season. So, if you have 6 seasons, and each "season" is composed of two months, then in order to estimate detection probability separately from occupancy, colonisation and extinction, you will need to code your observations for each single month: this would then give you two replicate observations for each season. Otherwise, if you treat the 6 seasons as replicates within a year (i.e, your closure period is 1 year), you will not be able to estimate seasonal occupancy, because by assumption, occupancy does not change within a year.

Best regards  -- Marc

